# Task 2 — Alert Funnel

In the previous room, I learned how to classify and triage alerts. This room helped me understand what happens after that triage and how the work of an L1 analyst contributes to preventing threats and stopping breaches.

The general path of alerts is:

```text
SIEM / EDR / Ticket Management Platform
                ↓
            SOC L1
                ↓
       Filter and investigate
                ↓
   Most alerts are handled at L1
                ↓
 Complex / threatening alerts
                ↓
            SOC L2
                ↓
 Deep investigation + remediation
                ↓
       Incident Response if needed
                ↓
             DFIR
```

The alert funnel shown in the room gives an example:

```text
100 SIEM Alerts
      ↓
   SOC L1
      ↓
10 True Positives
      ↓
   SOC L2
      ↓
 1 Incident
      ↓
   DFIR
```

This shows that a SOC can start with a very large number of alerts, but only a small number may turn into confirmed incidents requiring deeper investigation and incident response.

## Alert Reporting

Before closing an alert or passing it to L2, I may need to create an alert report.

Depending on the team's standards and the severity of the alert, a short analyst comment may not be enough.

For important alerts, especially **True Positives**, I may need to document the investigation in detail and include all relevant evidence.

A good report gives the next analyst enough context to understand what happened without having to repeat the entire investigation from the beginning.

## Alert Escalation

If a True Positive alert requires additional actions or deeper investigation, it should be escalated to an L2 analyst according to the team's procedures.

The alert report becomes very useful here.

L2 can read the report and understand:

- What happened
- What was investigated
- What evidence was found
- Why the L1 analyst reached the verdict
- What still needs to be investigated or remediated

This saves L2 time because they do not have to start the investigation completely from scratch.

## Communication

SOC analysts may also need to communicate with other departments during or after an investigation.

For example:

- Ask the IT team whether they really granted administrative privileges to a user.
- Contact HR to obtain information about a newly hired employee.
- Communicate with other teams when an incident affects their systems or users.

Good communication is therefore another important part of SOC work.

